\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can one uniform randomized logarithmic-word RAM algorithm find an explicit maximum-cardinality matching in every finite general graph with probability $\geq 2/3$, halting on every path within $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ instructions?

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can one uniform randomized logarithmic-word RAM algorithm find an explicit maximum-cardinality matching in every finite general graph with probability $\geq 2/3$, halting on every path within $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ instructions?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.01929}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

