\section*{Almost-linear maximum-cardinality matching}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 120; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that general simple graphs admit a uniform randomized algorithm producing a maximum matching in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ time on every path with success probability at least $2/3$, also yielding prescribed-degree subgraphs.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 120; 2026-09-24), factor-critical contraction and Hall reassignment maintain explicit matching states. Multiscale allocation ledgers control debt and resets; a cut-matching routing helper with conductance estimates keeps the required searches within the almost-linear budget.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 120; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 120; 2026-09-24).

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

